{"name":"Full of Life","hex":"#de5f2f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"full","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing the maximum possible amount that can fit in the space available.","Complete; with nothing omitted."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Fully; quite; very; thoroughly; completely; exactly; entirely."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/full"},{"term":"life","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of organisms preceding their death, characterized by biological processes such as metabolism and reproduction and distinguishing them from inanimate objects; the state of being alive and living.","The status possessed by any of a number of entities, including animals, plants, fungi, bacteria, and sometimes viruses, which have the properties of replication and metabolism."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To replace components whose operational lifetime has expired."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/life"}],"entries":[{"title":"Full of Life","blocks":[{"text":"Full of Life is a 1956 American comedy-drama film directed by Richard Quine and starring Judy Holliday and Richard Conte. It was nominated for an award by the Writers Guild of America in 1957."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/98/Full_of_Life_-_movie_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_of_Life","wikidata":"Q3902823"},{"title":"Life","blocks":[{"text":"Life is the capacity in matter, formed of one or more units called cells, for processes such as cell signalling, homeostasis, metabolism, cell growth, adaptation, response to stimuli, and reproduction. All life eventually reaches a state of death. Many philosophical definitions of living systems have been proposed, such as self-organising systems. Defining life is further complicated by viruses, which replicate only in host cells, and the possibility of extraterrestrial life, which could be very different from life on Earth. Life exists all over the Earth in air, water, and soil, with many ecosystems forming the biosphere. Some of these are harsh environments occupied only by extremophiles. The life in a particular ecosystem is called its biota."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Coral_reef..._South_end_of_my_area_%2814119221571%29.jpg/330px-Coral_reef..._South_end_of_my_area_%2814119221571%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Life","wikidata":"Q3"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/500px-Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/1280px-Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/500px-Male_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animal corpses, like this African buffalo, are recycled by the ecosystem, providing energy and nutrients for living organisms.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMale_Lion_and_Cub_Chitwa_South_Africa_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Adenovirus_transmission_electron_micrograph_B82-0142_lores.jpg/500px-Adenovirus_transmission_electron_micrograph_B82-0142_lores.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Ade